|~ The module of tag names: the core as a noun and the kind beneath
   every kind, the kinds with their constructors and the refusals of
   each, the tags of a quote's steps, and the verbs that live on a tag
   name. ~|

::qlang
  |~~ qlang is a pipeline language over values, and a language that
      describes itself in the same values: data, code, prose and
      failures are all values, so the pipeline that reshapes data also
      reads the description of the language. That description is a
      hypertext rooted here. Every name has a page; a page is a doc,
      prose whose quotes with no tag are laws that run and whose quotes
      under `::link` are links, queries to follow; `open` runs a link,
      so `::qlang | docs | first | links * open` answers every page this
      one names, in one query, and a vector of addresses reads the pages
      it names, `[::qlang/pipeline ::qlang/errors] * (docs | first |
      content)`.

      The forms. A query is a subject and its steps, each a command on
      the value the step before answered, `S | verb object`; a value
      never changes, a step answers a new one. `a | b` runs `b` on `a`;
      `a * b`, in the place of `|`, runs `b` on each element, several
      steps in parentheses; `/k`, `/0`, `/a/b` read a part; `(…)`
      groups steps on the value in the pipe; `:n v` names a value;
      `a !| b` runs `b` on an error alone. `42`, `"s"`, `:k`, `[a b]`,
      `{:k v}`, `#[a b]`, `null`, `true` are literals; `::T{…}` puts a
      value under the tag `::T`, the name of its kind; `~(…)` is code
      held as a value.

      Asking. `x | docs` answers the pages of a name or of the kind of a
      value, docs whose text is `content`, whose laws are `quotes` and
      whose links are `links`. ::link~(::qlang | manifest) lists the
      kinds; ::link~(::vec | spec | /verbs) lists the verbs of a kind by
      address, and ::link~(::qlang/any | spec | /verbs) those of every
      value; a verb in neither does not exist. `::vec/sort | docs` reads
      the page of one verb, `::vec/sort | runExamples` runs its laws,
      and `x !| explain` shows the page of what refused above an error.

      The concepts, in the order they rest on each other, each page
      naming its neighbours and the kinds that are it:
      ::link~(::qlang/values | docs | first) values, kinds and tags;
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | docs | first) the steps of a query;
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | docs | first) the verbs that live on kinds;
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | docs | first) the failure as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang/code | docs | first) code as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | docs | first) the pages and how to move
      through them; ::link~(::qlang/hosts | docs | first) the tool the
      language runs in and the budget of an answer.

      ~(::qlang | docs | first | links * open | count | gt 5)
      ~(::qlang/pipeline | docs | first | links * open | count | gt 0)
      ~([::qlang/pipeline ::qlang/errors] * (docs | first | content) | count | eq 2)
      ~([{:id 1 :tags [1 2]}] * {:id /id :n (/tags | count)} | eq [{:id 1 :n 2}])
      ~(::Box::Crate{:a 1} | type | eq ::Box)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::AddLeftNotNumberError)
  ~~|

::qlang/any
  |~~ The kind beneath every kind, where the verbs every value answers
      live: `type`, `eq`, `docs`, `print`, `explain`, `elide` and
      their kin. A verb that means the same for any value is written once
      here, and the walk from a name to its verb ends here, after the
      kinds of the subject. No value answers it as its `type`, since each
      has a kind of its own above it.
      ::link~(::qlang/any | spec | /verbs)
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | docs | first)

      ~(::qlang/any | spec | /verbs | has ::any/type)
      ~(5 | type | eq ::qlang/any | not)
  ~~|

::quote
  |~~ Code as a value, the value of a `~(…)` literal: a vector of steps
      under the code tag. Code is a value so that a verb takes code as its
      slot, `filter`, `sort`, `if`; a page holds laws that run and links
      that lead on; the trail of an error holds the steps it skipped,
      ready to replay; and a query is read, printed and built like any
      data. A literal step is the literal itself; a step that computes is
      a record, `::call`, `::proj`, `::bind` or `::tagged`; `*`, `!|`
      and parentheses wrap their step as `::each`, `::fail` and
      `::group`. `apply` runs a quote on the subject and `parse` prints
      it back. ::link~(::each | docs) ::link~(::call | docs)
      ::link~(::verb | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/code | docs | first)

      ~(~(1 | add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 3)
      ~(~(add 1) | eq ~(add 1))
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | payload | tag ::quote | eq ~(add 1 | mul 2))
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | filter ~(/name | eq :mul) | parse | eq "mul 2")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/quote}

::QuotePayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::quote` payload must be a Vec of steps.

      ~(
        ::quote{:not :steps}
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::QuotePayloadNotVecError ::map]
       )
  ~~|

::QuoteElementNotStepError
  |~~ Every element of a quote is a step: a literal, a quote, a
      container of steps, or a record or wrapper of code. A value
      with no reading as code, such as a tagged instance of
      another tag, is refused at the index it stands at.

      ~(
        [::Box[1]] | tag ::quote
        !| [type /index]
        |  eq [::QuoteElementNotStepError 0]
       )
  ~~|

::verb
  |~~ A verb, a quote under this tag: its leading declarations are its
      signature and the rest its body. Verbs live on kinds, the catalog
      itself is written as verbs, and a reader extends the language with
      the same form. A declaration whose body is a tag name is a slot the
      call fills, one whose body is a set of kinds takes a value of any of
      them and may be left out when `::null` is among them, and one whose
      body is any other literal takes that literal when it is left out;
      `* :name kind` gathers the modifiers that remain. `:subject` and
      `:returns` are the roles, the kind the verb serves and the kind it
      answers, `/` for its subject's own. A name bound to a verb runs it
      when it is mentioned, each modifier evaluated at the call and
      checked by the kind of its slot, and `~name` hands it on as code.
      ::link~(::spec | docs) ::link~(::quote | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | docs | first)

      ~(:double ::verb~(mul 2) | 3 | double | double | eq 12)
      ~(:least ::verb~(:n 1 | sort | take n) | [4 8 6] | [least (least 2)] | eq [[4] [4 6]])
      ~(:scale ::verb~(:by ::number | mul by) | [1 2] * scale / | eq [1 4])
      ~(:big ::verb~(gt 2) | [1 2 3 4] | filter ~big | eq [3 4])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/verb}

::VerbPayloadNotQuoteError
  |~~ `::verb` is a tag over a quote, and a payload of another kind is
      refused.

      ~(
        ::verb[1]
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::VerbPayloadNotQuoteError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|

::VerbSlotNameNotKeywordError
  |~~ A slot of a verb is named by a keyword, the name its body reads,
      so a declaration of a tag among the leading ones is refused.

      ~(
        ::verb~(::T 1 | add 1)
        !| [type /slot]
        |  eq [::VerbSlotNameNotKeywordError ::T]
       )
  ~~|

::VerbSlotWithoutBodyError
  |~~ The head of a verb is read before it runs, so a slot is declared
      by a kind, a set of kinds or a literal, and a leading declaration
      without a body is refused where the verb is made. A declaration
      whose body computes stands outside the head, the first step of the
      body.

      ~(
        ::verb~(:x |~~ How many. ~~| | add x)
        !| [type /slot]
        |  eq [::VerbSlotWithoutBodyError :x]
       )
  ~~|

::VerbRoleNotKindError
  |~~ The roles of a verb, `:subject` and `:returns`, are declared by a
      kind or a set of kinds, and `:returns` by `/` for the subject's
      own kind.

      ~(
        ::verb~(:returns 3 | add 1)
        !| [type /role]
        |  eq [::VerbRoleNotKindError :returns]
       )
  ~~|

::VerbSlotMissingError
  |~~ A slot declared by a kind alone is filled by the call, and a call
      that leaves it empty is refused by the name of the slot.

      ~(
        :m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | 5 | m
        !| [type /verbName /slot]
        |  eq [::VerbSlotMissingError :m :x]
       )
  ~~|

::VerbModifiersBeyondSlotsError
  |~~ A verb takes as many modifiers as it has slots, one more when
      the first is its subject, unless `*` gathers the ones that
      remain.

      ~(
        :m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | 5 | m 1 2 3
        !| [type /slotCount /actualCount]
        |  eq [::VerbModifiersBeyondSlotsError 1 3]
       )
  ~~|

::VerbSlotNotOfKindsError
  |~~ A slot declared by a set of kinds takes a value of one of them,
      reached through the tags the value carries, and refuses any
      other, since no one constructor reads it.

      ~(
        :m ::verb~(:limit #[::number ::null] | limit) | 5 | m "a"
        !| [type /slot /kinds]
        |  eq [::VerbSlotNotOfKindsError :limit #[::null ::number]]
       )
  ~~|

::VerbCodeNotQuoteError
  |~~ A slot of code takes a quote `~(…)` or a verb and nothing else,
      since a value is never read as code.

      ~(
        :run ::verb~(:code ::quote | apply code) | 5 | run 1
        !| [type /slot]
        |  eq [::VerbCodeNotQuoteError :code]
       )
  ~~|

::VerbWithoutBodyError
  |~~ A verb without a body declares a signature alone, a contract,
      and answers no call; the refusal names the residences of its
      name, where the verbs that answer it live.

      ~(
        :count ::verb~(:x ::number) | [1] | count 1
        !| [type /addresses]
        |  eq [::VerbWithoutBodyError #[::map/count ::set/count ::vec/count]]
       )
  ~~|

::spec
  |~~ The signature of a verb, its leading declarations as a quote under
      this tag with its subject filled, which `spec` answers for a verb:
      what a verb takes and answers is known before it is called, which is
      how a reader writes a call right the first time.
      ::link~(::vec/sort | spec)
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | docs | first)

      ~(:m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | :m | spec | type | eq ::spec)
      ~(:m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | :m | spec | payload * /name | eq [:subject :x])
  ~~|

::set
  |~~ The set, the vector in the one order without duplicates, the value
      of a `#[…]` literal, of `distinct` and of `keys`; `has` asks it
      for membership. A set is read as a vector wherever a vector is
      read; `filter`, `take`, `drop` and `flat` keep it, since its
      constructor re-runs after them, and an operand that imposes an
      order, `sort` or `reverse`, answers a vector. `*` over a set answers
      the vector of its images, one per element, and `distinct` after it
      makes a set of them again.
      ::link~(::vec | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | docs | first)

      ~(#[3 1 3] | eq #[1 3])
      ~([3 1 3] | tag ::set | eq ([3 1 3] | distinct))
      ~(#[2 1] | payload | eq [1 2])
      ~(#[3 1 2] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/set}

::SetPayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::set[…]` payload must be a Vec.

      ~(
        ::set{:not :a-vec}
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::SetPayloadNotVecError ::map]
       )
  ~~|

::error
  |~~ The kind of errors. A reader learning a tool fails often, and
      a failure is the moment when the knowledge it needs is cheapest to
      give, so a failure is a value that carries it: the facts of the
      failure, the path it took, and the tag of the site that refused,
      which names the page of the procedure. An error is a map of facts
      under `::error`, `::error{:k 1}`, a value like any other until it
      is raised, and the bang of a literal raises it, `!{:k 1}`. Every
      refusal is a tag of its own, named for what failed,
      `::AddLeftNotNumberError`, stacked over the error. `|` passes a
      raised error by, `*` stops on one, and `!|` opens it to that
      stack, so `!| type` reads the site, the verbs of this kind reach
      every error, and every step works on it as on any value: a fact is
      added and the error raised again, or it is recovered by the tag of
      its site. ::link~(::error | spec | /verbs)
      ::link~(::explanation | docs) ::link~(::fail | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | docs | first)

      ~(!{:detail "boom"} !| type | eq ::error)
      ~(::qlang/error | eq ::error)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::error | not)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| payload | type | eq ::error)
      ~(::error{:k 1} | /k | eq 1)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| union {:request "r-1"} | raise !| /request | eq "r-1")
      ~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::AddLeftNotNumberError) ~(0) ~(raise) | eq 0)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::Other) ~(0) ~(raise) !| type | eq ::AddLeftNotNumberError)
  ~~|

::elision
  |~~ The kind of the marker of what a view left out. A
      reader's context only grows, and whatever an answer adds is read
      again on every later step, so a host answers within a budget, and
      the marker keeps the way back: `:size` counts the elements, the
      entries or the characters it stands for, `:read` is the quote that
      applied to the value taken answers the part left out, and `:head`
      holds the first characters of a string. `elide` writes markers,
      and a reader continues its query by `:read` to see the rest.
      ::link~(::any/elide | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/hosts | docs | first)

      ~(::elision | docs | count | eq 1)
      ~({:s "a long line of text to leave out"} | elide 20 | /s | /size | eq 32)
  ~~|

::link
  |~~ The kind of a link, a query a page names for its reader to follow,
      written in the prose of a doc as a quote under `::link`.
      Links are what make the pages a hypertext: a page names the pages
      and the answers that go deeper, and a reader reaches them by running
      the query the link holds, or by `apply` over the link. A quote with
      no tag is a law, which answers true; a link answers without an
      error, and the suite follows every link of the catalog, so none
      goes dead. A host may stack its own tag over a link.
      ::link~(::qlang | docs | first | links)
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | docs | first)

      ~(|~~ ::link~(::qlang | manifest) ~~| | links | first | type | eq ::link)
      ~(|~~ ::Host::link~(1) ~~| | links | count | eq 1)
  ~~|

::explanation
  |~~ The kind of what `explain` answers: the page `docs` reads
      for a value under `:doc`, then the value itself under `:value`, so
      it prints as the page above what it explains, an error
      raised, with its bang. It is the cord from a value to its
      procedure in one answer; `doc` and `value` answer its parts.
      ::link~(::error | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | docs | first)

      ~("x" | add 1 !| explain | type | eq ::explanation)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| explain | value !| type | eq ::AddLeftNotNumberError)
  ~~|

::binding
  |~~ The kind of the record a declaration writes into its scope. A name
      is a record that carries its page and its source, so what is named
      can be listed and asked: `:name`, the keyword or the tag it binds,
      `:docs`, one doc per doc of its slot, `:value`, `:source`, the
      quote of the declaring step, and `:module`, the source it came
      from. `env` answers the records of the scope, a name reads the value
      its record holds, and the axes project the record, so `env | /x |
      docs` answers the docs of `x`. A value `use` or a host binds is a
      binding without a doc and without a source. ::link~(::bind | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | docs | first)

      ~(:x |~~ One. ~~| 1 | env | /x | /value | eq 1)
      ~(:x |~~ One. ~~| 1 | env | /x | docs | first | content | eq " One. ")
      ~(:x 1 | env | /x | /name | eq :x)
      ~({:k 2} | use | env | /k | /source | eq null)
  ~~|

::null
  |~~ The kind of null, the absence of a value; a slot a call may leave
      empty declares `::null` among its kinds. A kind of the core is
      named under its prefix and written short, `::qlang/null` reading as
      `::null`, and its constructor reads a payload of the kind as the
      value itself. ::link~(::verb | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | docs | first)

      ~(null | type | eq ::null)
      ~(::qlang/null | eq ::null)
      ~(::null(null) | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/null}

::NullPayloadNotNullError
  |~~ `::null(…)` payload must be null.
      ~(
        ::null(1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::NullPayloadNotNullError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::boolean
  |~~ The kind of `true` and `false`, the answer of a question about a
      value: `filter`, `every` and `if` take a quote that answers one,
      and `false !| true` after steps answers whether they failed.
      ::link~(::boolean | spec | /verbs)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | docs | first)

      ~(true | type | eq ::boolean)
      ~(::boolean(false) | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/boolean}

::BooleanPayloadNotBooleanError
  |~~ `::boolean(…)` payload must be a boolean.
      ~(
        ::boolean(1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::BooleanPayloadNotBooleanError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::number
  |~~ The kind of a number, a finite double: an infinity or NaN is no
      value, so a step whose result leaves the domain answers an error.
      No kind reads text, so `::number(5)` is `5` and `::number"5"` is
      refused; `parseJson` reads a number out of a string.
      ::link~(::number | spec | /verbs)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | docs | first)

      ~(5 | type | eq ::number)
      ~(::qlang/number(5) | eq 5)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/number}

::NumberPayloadNotNumberError
  |~~ `::number(…)` payload must be a number.
      ~(
        ::number"5"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::NumberPayloadNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::string
  |~~ The kind of a string, text as it came. A JSON string arrives as a
      string, and `keyword` makes one a keyword where a key is wanted,
      since the keys of a map are keywords. Its verbs work on the text
      whole or by its lines, and `lines` breaks it into the vector a
      pipeline reads in pieces.
      ::link~(::string | spec | /verbs) ::link~(::keyword | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | docs | first)

      ~("x" | type | eq ::string)
      ~(::string"x" | eq "x")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/string}

::StringPayloadNotStringError
  |~~ `::string…` payload must be a string.
      ~(
        ::string(1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::StringPayloadNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::keyword
  |~~ The kind of a keyword, `:k`, a name that stands for itself. It is
      apart from the string so that no name is read by its look: every
      key of a map is a keyword, `/k` reads a field by it, and a keyword
      names a binding of the scope; `keyword` makes one of a string and
      a string of one. ::link~(::map | docs) ::link~(::binding | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | docs | first)

      ~(:k | type | eq ::keyword)
      ~(::keyword:k | eq :k)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/keyword}

::KeywordPayloadNotKeywordError
  |~~ `::keyword…` payload must be a keyword.
      ~(
        ::keyword"k"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::KeywordPayloadNotKeywordError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::tag
  |~~ The kind of a tag name, `::Kind` taken as a value. A tag is the name
      of a kind, the interpretation of the value it stands on, and the
      model of the language hangs on it: a value carries its kind as the
      tag over it, a name finds its verb through that tag, and the tag
      names the page of the kind, so `type | docs` reads the page of a
      value's kind. Tags stack over any payload and read from the outside
      in; behaviour does not travel with a value, it is found again
      through the tag wherever the value goes.
      ::link~(::Box::Crate{:a 1} | type) ::link~(::qlang | manifest)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | docs | first)

      ~(::Box | type | eq ::tag)
      ~(::Even {:impl ~(if (eq 2) ~(/) ~(!{:reason :odd}))} | ::Even(3) !| /reason | eq :odd)
      ~(::tag::Box | eq ::Box)
      ~(5 | type | docs | count | gt 0)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/tag}

::TagPayloadNotTagError
  |~~ `::tag…` payload must be a tag name.
      ~(
        ::tag:k
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::TagPayloadNotTagError ::keyword]
       )
  ~~|

::vec
  |~~ The kind of a vector, the value of a `[…]` literal, and the shape a
      long value takes: its parts have an index, a size and a slice,
      `drop` and `take`, so the rest of a long answer is the same query
      with a tail, and `*` runs a step on each element. A set, a quote
      and a doc are vectors under their tags, read as vectors wherever a
      vector is read; over them the constructor answers the vector of
      their elements, and `::vec[1 2]` reads as `[1 2]`.
      ::link~(::vec | spec | /verbs) ::link~(::each | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | docs | first)

      ~([1 2] | type | eq ::vec)
      ~(::qlang/vec[1 2] | eq [1 2])
      ~(#[2 1] | tag ::vec | eq [1 2])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/vec}

::VecPayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::vec[…]` payload must be a Vec.
      ~(
        ::vec{:a 1}
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::VecPayloadNotVecError ::map]
       )
  ~~|

::map
  |~~ The kind of a map, the value of a `{…}` literal: named facts under
      keywords, the shape a JSON object arrives in. A map literal is also
      the shape an answer takes, each value a group run on the subject,
      `{:id /id :n (/tags | count)}`; `/k` reads a field, and `*` over
      a map runs a step on each value under its key.
      ::link~(::map | spec | /verbs) ::link~(::proj | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | docs | first)

      ~({:a 1} | type | eq ::map)
      ~(::map{:a 1} | eq {:a 1})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/map}

::MapPayloadNotMapError
  |~~ `::map{…}` payload must be a Map.
      ~(
        ::map[1]
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::MapPayloadNotMapError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|

::doc
  |~~ The kind of a doc, prose as a value, the value of a doc literal:
      text that carries quotes of qlang. It is how the language says what
      it is: every page is a doc, `docs` answers the pages of a name, and
      because a doc is a value its code is checked and the next step can
      take it apart. A quote with no tag is a law of the page, which
      `runExamples` holds to answer true; a quote under `::link` is a
      link, a query a reader follows. `content` answers its text,
      `segments` its parts, `quotes` its laws and `links` its links.
      ::link~(::doc | spec | /verbs) ::link~(::link | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | docs | first)

      ~(::number/add | docs | first | type | eq ::doc)
      ~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | segments | count | eq 3)
      ~(["a" ~(add 1)] | tag ::doc | eq (|~~a~(add 1)~~|))
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/doc}

::DocPayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::doc` payload must be a Vec of segments.

      ~(
        ::doc"x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::DocPayloadNotVecError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::DocElementNotSegmentError
  |~~ Every element of a doc is a segment, a string of prose or a
      quote, and another value is refused at the index it stands at.

      ~(
        ["a" 1] | tag ::doc
        !| [type /index]
        |  eq [::DocElementNotSegmentError 1]
       )
  ~~|

::call
  |~~ The step of a command, `verb object`: the name finds its verb
      through the kind of the subject and runs it with the modifiers.
      As a record, `:name` is the keyword of the name it calls and
      `:args` the quote of each argument's pipeline when the call has
      parentheses. Like every record, a `::call` is the step its own text
      reads back as: the constructor checks the fields against the schema,
      prints the step and reads it back. ::link~(::quote | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | docs | first)

      ~(~(filter ~(gt 1)) | payload | eq [::call{:name :filter :args [~(gt 1)]}])
      ~(~(count) | first | /name | eq :count)
      ~({:name :filter :args [~(gt 1)]} | tag ::call | [/] | tag ::quote | eq ~(filter ~(gt 1)))
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/call}

::CallPayloadNotSchemaError
  |~~ A ::call payload is a Map of `:name` a keyword and `:args`
      a Vec of quotes; `:field` names the field that does not fit,
      null when the payload is no Map.

      ~(
        ::call{:name 42}
        !| [type /field]
        |  eq [::CallPayloadNotSchemaError :name]
       )
  ~~|

::CallReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The ::call prints as text that reads back as another step,
      or as none: `:printed` is that text.

      ~(
        ::call{:name :"a b"}
        !| [type /printed]
        |  eq [::CallReadBackDiffersError "a b"]
       )
  ~~|

::proj
  |~~ The step of a projection, `/a/0/b`: it reads a part of the value in
      the pipe by keys and indices, a negative index from the end, and a
      miss answers an error, never null, so a misspelled field is found
      where it is read. `/` is the value itself. As a record, `:path` is
      the keys and indices it walks, `[]` for `/`.
      ::link~({:a {:b [5 6]}} | /a/b/-1)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | docs | first)

      ~(~(/items/0/name) | first | /path | eq [:items 0 :name])
      ~(~(/) | first | /path | eq [])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/proj}

::ProjPayloadNotSchemaError
  |~~ A ::proj payload is a Map of `:path` a Vec of keys and
      integers.

      ~(
        ::proj{:path "items"}
        !| [type /field]
        |  eq [::ProjPayloadNotSchemaError :path]
       )
  ~~|

::ProjReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The ::proj prints as text that reads back as another step: a
      key that reads as an index is written as the index.

      ~(
        ::proj{:path [:"0"]}
        !| [type /printed]
        |  eq [::ProjReadBackDiffersError "/\"0\""]
       )
  ~~|

::bind
  |~~ The step of a declaration, `:name body`: it names a value for the
      steps after it, in the scope where it stands, and a group, an
      element of a literal and the body of `*` each open a scope of their
      own, so a name never leaks out of the fork that declared it. As a
      record, `:name` is the keyword or tag it binds, `:docs` its prose
      and `:body` the step of its body. ::link~(::binding | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | docs | first)

      ~(~(:inc add 1) | first | [/name /body/name] | eq [:inc :add])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/bind}

::BindPayloadNotSchemaError
  |~~ A ::bind payload is a Map of `:name` a keyword or a tag,
      `:docs` a Vec of strings and `:body` a step.

      ~(
        ::bind{:name :inc :docs [1]}
        !| [type /field]
        |  eq [::BindPayloadNotSchemaError :docs]
       )
  ~~|

::BindReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The ::bind prints as text that reads back as another step: a
      declaration binds a body or docs, and a body that is a keyword
      or a tag is written as a group.

      ~(
        ::bind{:name :inc}
        !| [type /printed]
        |  eq [::BindReadBackDiffersError ":inc"]
       )
  ~~|

::tagged
  |~~ The step of a constructor invocation, `::T<payload>`: it puts a
      payload under the tag `::T` through the tag's constructor, which
      checks or builds the value. As a record, `:tag` is the tag and
      `:payload` the step of its payload. ::link~(::tag | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | docs | first)

      ~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | [/tag /payload] | eq [::Box [1 2]])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/tagged}

::TaggedPayloadNotSchemaError
  |~~ A ::tagged payload is a Map of `:tag` a tag and `:payload` a
      step.

      ~(
        ::tagged{:tag :Box :payload [1]}
        !| [type /field]
        |  eq [::TaggedPayloadNotSchemaError :tag]
       )
  ~~|

::TaggedReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The ::tagged prints as text that reads back as another step: a
      payload that opens with a name, a digit or a slash is written
      as a group.

      ~(
        ::tagged{:tag ::Box :payload 42}
        !| [type /printed]
        |  eq [::TaggedReadBackDiffersError "::Box42"]
       )
  ~~|

::each
  |~~ The step `* body`, the distribute: it runs its body on each
      element of a vector or each value of a map and answers the results
      in their place, the map of other languages; the first element whose
      body fails is its answer, the elements after it left unrun,
      and a body that recovers, `* (f !| 0)`, keeps every element. `*`
      stands in the place of `|`, and several steps of a body stand in
      parentheses. As a record it wraps the quote of its body.
      ::link~(::vec | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | docs | first)

      ~(~(* add 1) | first | payload | eq ~(add 1))
      ~(~(* (add 1 | mul 2)) | first | payload | count | eq 2)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/each}

::EachPayloadNotQuoteError
  |~~ The payload of ::each is the quote of its body.

      ~(
        42 | tag ::each
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::EachPayloadNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::EachReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The ::each prints as text that reads back as another step, or
      as none: a body holds at least one step.

      ~(
        ~() | tag ::each
        !| [type /printed]
        |  eq [::EachReadBackDiffersError "* ()"]
       )
  ~~|

::fail
  |~~ The step `!| step`, the fail track: it runs its step on an error
      alone, opened to its facts, and passes any other value by, so it is
      where a failure is read, recovered or raised again, while `|`
      passes the error by. As a record it wraps the quote of that one
      step. ::link~(::error | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | docs | first)

      ~(~(!| /kind) | first | payload | eq ~(/kind))
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/fail}

::FailPayloadNotQuoteError
  |~~ The payload of ::fail is the quote of the step it wraps.

      ~(
        42 | tag ::fail
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::FailPayloadNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::FailReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The ::fail prints as text that reads back as another step: it
      wraps one step, and a pipeline under `!|` is written as a
      group.

      ~(
        ~(a | b) | tag ::fail
        !| [type /printed]
        |  eq [::FailReadBackDiffersError "!| a | b"]
       )
  ~~|

::group
  |~~ The step `(pipeline)`, a group: it runs its steps on the value in
      the pipe and answers their result, its names left inside. Each part
      of a literal is such a group, which is why a literal is the shape an
      answer takes, `[(add 1) (mul 2)]`. As a record it wraps the quote
      of its pipeline. ::link~(::bind | docs)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | docs | first)

      ~(~((add 1 | mul 2)) | first | payload | count | eq 2)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/group}

::GroupPayloadNotQuoteError
  |~~ The payload of ::group is the quote of its pipeline.

      ~(
        42 | tag ::group
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::GroupPayloadNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::GroupReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The ::group prints as text that reads back as another step, or
      as none: a group holds at least one step.

      ~(
        ~() | tag ::group
        !| [type /printed]
        |  eq [::GroupReadBackDiffersError "()"]
       )
  ~~|

|~ The comparisons of tag names. Each resides on `::qlang/tag`,
   the kind of the module that declares it, and compares two tag names
   by their names in the one order. ~|

:gt
  |~~ Whether the subject's name follows the name of the tag the call
      gives.

      ~(::b | gt ::a | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::tag | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/gt})

:gte
  |~~ Whether the subject's name follows or equals the name of the tag
      the call gives.

      ~(::b | gte ::b | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::tag | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/gte})

:lt
  |~~ Whether the subject's name precedes the name of the tag the call
      gives.

      ~(::a | lt ::b | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::tag | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lt})

:lte
  |~~ Whether the subject's name precedes or equals the name of the
      tag the call gives.

      ~(::a | lte ::a | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::tag | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lte})

:manifest
  |~~ Asked of a noun, answers the set of what lies below it in
      the tree of names: the nouns under its path and the
      addresses of the verbs that live on it. `::qlang | manifest`
      lists the nouns of the core and of the hosts, `::number |
      manifest` the verbs of numbers, and a refusal is reached
      from the place it guards, `::number/add | spec | /throws`.
      For what one binding does reach for the axis trio
      (`::vec/count | source` / `| docs` / `| examples`).

      ~(::number | manifest | has ::number/add)
      ~(::qlang | manifest | has ::vec)
      ~(::qlang | manifest * manifest | flat | has ::vec/count)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::set | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/manifest})

::ManifestSubjectNotTagError
  |~~ `manifest` is asked of a noun, a tag name in the subject
      position.

      ~(
        5 | manifest
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ManifestSubjectNotTagError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:runExamples
  |~~ The results of the examples of the declaration the tag name
      reads, a verb by its address among them.

      ~(::number/add | runExamples | first | /ok | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::vec | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/runExamples})
